/* book Worked example 2: Q: A solenoid has N = 500 turns and a pole face of 1 cm². Using F ≈ μ₀N²I²A ÷ (2g²), find the pull at 1 A with a 1.0 mm air gap and with the plunger seated (0.2 mm gap). What current holds the seated plunger with the same pull as at pull-in? If the coil is 6 Ω on 12 V, what PWM duty gives that hold current?
   1. Data in SI units: μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ = 1.2566 × 10⁻⁶ H/m, A = 1 × 10⁻⁴ m², N² = 250 000.
   2. At g = 1.0 mm: F = 1.2566 × 10⁻⁶ × 250 000 × 1² × 10⁻⁴ ÷ (2 × (10⁻³)²) = 3.142 × 10⁻⁵ ÷ 2 × 10⁻⁶ = 15.7 N.
   3. At g = 0.2 mm the gap term is 25 times smaller, so F = 15.7 × 25 = 393 N at the same 1 A.
   4. Force goes as (I ÷ g)², so the same 15.7 N at 0.2 mm needs I = 1 × 0.2 ÷ 1.0 = 0.2 A.
   5. Full-on current is 12 ÷ 6 = 2 A; with Iavg ≈ D Vb ÷ Rc, a hold of 0.2 A needs D = 0.2 × 6 ÷ 12 = 0.10 (10 %).
   6. Check on heat: the coil dissipates I²R = 2² × 6 = 24 W full on but only about 0.2² × 6 = 0.24 W at the hold current, which is why injectors and valves use peak-and-hold drive.
   Answer: 15.7 N at 1.0 mm and 393 N at 0.2 mm (both at 1 A); 0.2 A holds the seated plunger with 15.7 N, which a 10 % PWM duty gives on 12 V. */
