/* book exam traps: A relay coil and a solenoid coil are inductors: removing current without a freewheeling diode causes a high voltage spike and can destroy the driver. | Pull-in current is larger than hold current. Do not use the pull-in current for continuous holding; the coil overheats. | A stepper motor is open-loop. It can lose steps under overload or fast acceleration; counting pulses does not guarantee position. | In an H-bridge, switching both transistors on one leg at the same time causes shoot-through. Dead time is required. | Microstepping gives smoother motion, not more holding torque. The torque increment gained per microstep is smaller than for a full step; the motor's maximum holding torque stays the same. */
